    'tour.1.title': 'SPHEREx',
    'tour.1.text': `The *${SPHEREX}* will survey the entire sky in infrared light. During its planned two-year mission, it will collect data on more than 450 million galaxies and over 100 million stars in the Milky Way to explore the origins of the universe.`,
    'tour.2.title': 'What is SPHEREx looking for?',
    'tour.2.text': 'SPHEREx maps the sky in 102 infrared bands. By measuring galaxy distances and the light they emit, it helps astronomers investigate the universe’s early expansion. It also studies water and other ices in cold, star-forming clouds across the Milky Way.',
    'tour.3.title': 'Why look at the same place twice?',
    'tour.3.text': 'Because objects can move or change. In 1930, Clyde Tombaugh discovered Pluto by comparing two photographs of the same patch of sky and spotting a dot that had shifted. SPHEREx repeatedly observes hundreds of millions of stars and galaxies, far too many for one team to inspect. The more people who help compare the images, the more likely we are to spot something interesting.',
    'tour.px.title': 'Planet X',
    'tour.px.text': 'Tombaugh found Pluto while searching for a “Planet X” that had been predicted beyond Neptune. Today the name belongs to another prediction: in 2016, astronomers at Caltech showed that the most distant small bodies of the solar system are aligned in a way that the gravity of an unseen planet would explain. By NASA’s account it would have 5 to 10 times the mass of Earth, orbit 20 to 30 times farther out than Neptune and take 10,000 to 20,000 years to go around the Sun. *Nobody has seen it: it remains a hypothesis.*',
    'tour.faint.title': 'Could SPHEREx see it?',
